{"t":1789815433,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f8433f5e-03af-4f56-8d0f-72dfd0b379fb","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-19T07:57:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 190757\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n357 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo major changes have been made to the previous forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Slightly cooler and drier conditions are forecast for\n  today, before showers and thunderstorms return Sunday.\n\n- 2) Daily chances for showers persist into next week along \n  with cooler temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slightly cooler and drier conditions are \nforecast for today, before showers and thunderstorms return \nSunday.\n\nHigh pressure currently centered across southern Quebec will\ncontinue to build southeastward over the course of the day\ntoday, eventually reaching the Gulf of Maine by this evening. \nAs the high shifts further east, winds will gradually shift from\nout of the north/northeast this morning to out of the east and \nthen southeast this afternoon. Persistent cool/dry advection has\nenabled dewpoints to drop into the upper 50s to mid 60s as of \nearly this morning. With this somewhat drier airmass in place, \nzonal flow aloft, and the influence of the high building to our\nnorth, conditions are expected to remain dry through the day \ntoday. Temperatures are forecast to climb into the 70s to near \n80 beneath mostly cloudy skies.\n\nHigh pressure will shift further offshore tomorrow while a\nshortwave disturbance tracks eastward across southern Ontario\nand Quebec. Winds at the surface will turn southerly in response\nto the high moving further offshore, while winds aloft turn\nwesterly in response to the shortwave passing to our north. The\nnet effect of these wind shifts will be to draw a warmer, more\nhumid airmass back into the area from the south and west.\nTemperatures are forecast to climb well into the 80s with\ndewpoints jumping back into the lower 70s. Model guidance \nsuggests that chances for showers and potentially a few \nthunderstorms will increase tomorrow afternoon into tomorrow \nnight as a cold front approaches from the north, moving into the\nwarm and humid airmass. This cold front will eventually move \nthrough the area Sunday night, causing winds to shift to out of \nthe north to northeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers persist into next \nweek along with cooler temperatures. \n\nUpper-level ridging breaks down early next week, with multiple \nshortwave disturbances moving across the Mid-Atlantic in the days \nfollowing. Mid-level moisture brought in from the south will \noverrun moist northeasterly flow off the Atlantic, leading to\ndaily chances for showers. Temperatures remain somewhat uncertain\ndue to model discrepancy in cloud cover, but they continue to \nfavor the cooler side of guidance given a possible cold air \ndamming surface pattern. \n\nWell below average temperatures could last through midweek as nearby \ndisturbances continue to bring rain and showers to the area. Towards \nthe bay and coastlines, wind gusts could remain elevated through \nlate next week as these disturbances shift offshore. Overall, \ncoastal flooding could become something to watch out for next\nweek. See the coastal flooding section below for more details. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through the day today as winds\ngradually shift from the northeast to the southeast. MVFR\nceilings may be possible later tonight, with gradual improvement\nback to VFR tomorrow morning. Winds will be out of the south\ntomorrow, and a few showers and thunderstorms may be possible\ntomorrow afternoon into tomorrow night as a cold front\napproaches from the north. Winds eventually shift to out of the\nnorth later Sunday night as the front moves through. Sub-VFR\nceilings appear likely behind the front in northeasterly flow on\nMonday.\n\nSub-VFR ceilings likely continue on Tuesday and Wednesday, along\nwith showers and gusty northeasterly winds.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions continue over the main stem of\nthe Bay within northeasterly flow early this morning. Winds are\nexpected to decrease after sunrise and then gradually shift\naround to out of the east. Winds pick up out of the east to\nsoutheast tonight, with SCA conditions possible. Low-end SCA\nlevel winds may linger into Sunday within southerly flow across\nthe wider waters of the Bay. A cold front will move over the\nwaters Sunday night, causing winds to shift to out of the north.\nSCA conditions may be possible in the surge of northerly winds\nbehind the front Sunday night. SCA conditions will likely\ncontinue within northeasterly flow on Monday, and then easterly\nflow Monday night. \n\nHeading into the middle of next week, winds could approach Gale\nconditions between Tuesday and Wednesday within northeasterly \nflow as a strong area of high pressure builds to our north.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies will gradually increase as winds shift from the\nnortheast this morning to east this afternoon, southeast\ntonight, and then south tomorrow. Annapolis could come close to\nMinor Flooding tomorrow, but for now the forecast has them just\nbelow in Action stage. After a brief drop in anomalies behind a\ncold front Sunday night, anomalies should gradually increase\nonce again next week within onshore flow. At least Minor\nflooding appears likely during the middle of next week, and\nModerate flooding can't be completely ruled out.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for \n     ANZ530>533-539>541.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for ANZ534-\n     537-543.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 5 PM this afternoon to 1 AM EDT \n     Sunday for ANZ534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KJP/KVH\nAVIATION...KJP/KVH\nMARINE...KJP/KVH\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789815410,"days":{"2026-09-19":{"hi":80,"lo":66,"pop":16,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":85,"lo":64,"pop":71,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":73,"lo":60,"pop":40,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":67,"lo":56,"pop":55,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-09-23":{"hi":63,"lo":56,"pop":46,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-24":{"hi":64,"lo":55,"pop":46,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-25":{"hi":69,"lo":54,"pop":18,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789812000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.7,9.2,9.0,8.9,9.0,10.0,10.0,5.0,4.0,10.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0],"spd":[9,9,7,7,7,7,6,5,5,5,6,6,6,7,7,7,6,6,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,6,7,9,9,10,10,10,10,9,9,8,6,5,4,3,2,2,2,3,5,6,8],"gst":[13,11,12,12,11,11,10,8,7,7,8,9,10,11,13,13,13,12,12,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,12,14,14,15,15,15,15,15,14,12,11,10,9,7,6,6,7,8,10,13,15],"dir":[30,40,50,50,50,60,60,70,70,80,80,90,100,100,110,110,120,120,120,130,130,140,150,150,150,160,170,180,180,190,200,190,190,200,200,200,200,200,200,210,240,260,280,310,330,340,350,350],"pop":[0,0,1,1,0,2,2,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,7,7,8,10,10,12,7,6,6,8,5,5,7,7,4,14,7,0,4,5,20,18,5,32,70,70,70,70,70,70,30,30,30,30],"temp":[18.9,17.8,18.9,20,22.2,22.8,24.4,25,25.6,25.6,25.6,26.1,25,24.4,23.3,21.7,20.6,20,20,20,20,20,20,20,19.4,20,20.6,22.2,23.9,25,26.1,27.2,27.8,27.8,2